---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About Dr. DuBose — DuBose, M.D." active="about">

  <!-- 1 · Introduction -->
  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="about-grid">
        <div class="photo-slot">
          [Portrait: Dr.&nbsp;DuBose near the upright piano, natural light]
        </div>
        <div>
          <h1>About me</h1>
          <p style="margin-top:20px">
            I&rsquo;m Logan DuBose — a physician and researcher. I&rsquo;ve
            spent my career in two places at once: practicing primary care,
            and leading research on why healthcare so often fails the people
            who need it most. This practice is where those two careers meet:
            one doctor, a small panel, and enough time to do medicine
            properly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 · Why I practice this way -->
  <section class="band-warm">
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>Why I practice this way</h2>
      <p>
        My path isn&rsquo;t the usual one, so let me tell it to you straight.
        I earned my M.D. at the Texas A&amp;M College of Medicine and
        completed an internal medicine internship at George Washington
        University. Then, instead of continuing down the standard hospital
        track, I made a bet on a question that had bothered me through all of
        training: why does a system with this much medicine in it leave so
        many people feeling unseen?
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">
        I spent the next years working on that question — leading research
        funded by the National Institutes of Health on aging, caregiving, and
        access to care, and building tools to help families navigate a system
        that seems designed to be unnavigable. The whole time, I kept
        practicing primary care, including as a volunteer physician at a
        charity health center, where I cared for people the system had priced
        out entirely.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">
        Here is what years of studying healthcare&rsquo;s failures taught me:
        most people are not failed by a lack of medicine. They are failed by
        fragmentation — fifteen-minute visits, results that arrive without
        explanation, five doctors and no one holding the whole picture. I
        have measured that problem, published on it, and watched it happen to
        people I cared about.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">
        This practice is my answer. Not a company, not a platform — a return
        to the simplest unit of medicine: one physician and one person, with
        enough time between them. I keep the panel small and the overhead
        near zero so the price can stay at $100 a month and the visits can be
        as long as they need to be. I practice alongside my continuing
        research on healthy aging — each one makes me better at the other.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 · About experience — stated plainly -->
  <section>
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>About experience — stated plainly</h2>
      <p>
        I believe trust starts with precision about qualifications, so here
        is mine without varnish: I completed medical school and an internal
        medicine internship, and I have practiced primary care part-time for
        years while leading research full-time. I have not spent decades in
        an exam room, and I won&rsquo;t pretend otherwise.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:16px">
        What I promise instead is a way of practicing: I work from evidence.
        I look things up rather than guess. I think before I prescribe. I say
        &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; when I don&rsquo;t — and then I find
        out. And I refer early and without ego, because knowing when a
        problem needs a specialist, an urgent care visit, or an emergency
        room is one of the most important skills in primary care. This
        practice is built around those habits: time to think, time to
        investigate, and no financial pressure to do anything but what your
        situation needs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 · Training and credentials -->
  <section class="band-warm">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Training and credentials</h2>
      <div class="cred-table">
        <div class="row"><b>M.D.</b><p>Texas A&amp;M College of Medicine</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Internship</b><p>Internal medicine, George Washington University</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Licensure</b><p>Virginia (unrestricted), DEA-registered; Texas licensure [at launch]</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Clinical practice</b><p>Part-time primary care; volunteer general practitioner, Fredericksburg Christian Health Center</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Research</b><p>Chief Research Officer, Olera; NIH-funded research on aging, caregiving, and access to care; Affiliate Faculty, Texas A&amp;M School of Public Health, Center for Community Health &amp; Aging</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>In progress</b><p>Lifestyle medicine certification, International Board of Lifestyle Medicine</p></div>
        <div class="row"><b>Education</b><p>B.S. Biochemistry, The University of Texas at Austin; M.B.A., Texas A&amp;M University</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 · How I practice -->
  <section>
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>How I practice</h2>
      <div class="pay-rows">
        <div class="row">
          <b>Evidence first</b>
          <p>I recommend what good evidence supports. When the evidence is
          thin or mixed, I say so, and we decide together with that honesty
          on the table.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>I explain my reasoning</b>
          <p>You will know not just what I recommend but why — and what
          I&rsquo;d expect to happen if we chose differently.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>I say &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know&rdquo; when I don&rsquo;t</b>
          <p>Then I find out, or I find the person who knows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>I refer without ego</b>
          <p>When someone else is the right doctor for part of your care,
          you&rsquo;ll be in their office — with me still watching the whole
          picture.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="row">
          <b>Your decisions are yours</b>
          <p>My job is to make sure they are informed, unrushed, and
          genuinely yours.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6 · Outside the practice -->
  <section class="band-warm">
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>Outside the practice</h2>
      <p>
        I&rsquo;m married to a pediatrician I met in medical school — our
        house settles most medical debates by specialty. I&rsquo;ve played
        jazz since high school and still do; I read and write; and my wife
        and I will take any excuse to travel, find live comedy, or get to a
        beach. Some of the most meaningful medicine I&rsquo;ve done has been
        on medical mission trips, which taught me how much good a doctor can
        do with very little besides time and attention. That lesson is half
        of why this practice exists.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7 · CTA -->
  <section class="band-tint">
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>The best way to know if we&rsquo;d work well together is to talk.</h2>
      <p>
        Thirty minutes, by video, free — and if I&rsquo;m not the right fit,
        I&rsquo;ll say so and point you toward someone good.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:26px"><a class="btn" href="/book">Book a free conversation</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
